DECISIONS 403, CODEGEN.md §3.5, §7.9, IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md §4.5: the defines of the project come from every scanned package, so selecting b alone, a package that does not read a, gives the finding it gives in a whole run: a's define alpha is b's enumerator alpha, E8005 foreignDefine (a is neither selected nor loaded).
-- select --
b
-- check-only --
-- project.canon --
project acme {
  canon: "0.1"
  roots {
    out: "out"
  }
}
-- a/a.canon --
/// A.
package a

/// Kinds; the defines keep the names.
enum Kind @cpp(defines: "") {
  /// One.
  alpha
  /// Two.
  beta
}

emit cpp { out: "@out/a", namespace: "a", mode: types }
-- b/b.canon --
/// B.
package b

/// Tones.
enum Tone {
  /// Spelled like a's define.
  alpha
  /// Another.
  gamma
}

emit cpp { out: "@out/b", namespace: "b", mode: types }
-- findings.txt --
error[E8005]  b/b.canon:7:3
  alpha is declared for b.Tone.alpha and written as a #define for a.Kind.alpha, in a package this one does not read, so its headers cannot guard it; rename b.Tone.alpha with @cpp(name:), or use a type of that package here

1 error, 0 warnings in 1 package (…)
